{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem concerns an append-only event log, which inherently imposes a total order by append position (entry N precedes entry N+1). The target's central claim is that 'the replay function must be a semilattice fold,' requiring commutativity so that reordered entries converge. But in an append-only log, entries are not reordered during replay—they are replayed in append order. The target's own countercondition concedes this: 'If the log imposes a total order (e.g., sequence numbers), commutativity is unnecessary and only idempotency of the processor suffices.' Since the problem specifies an append-only log (which provides total ordering by construction), the semilattice-fold requirement—including commutativity—is too strong and directly contradicted by the target's own admitted countercondition. The 'real requirement' as stated is therefore not the real requirement for the scenario the problem actually describes.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The target mislabels a deterministic writer as 'idempotent.' It claims 'a writer that appends a deterministic hash of the payload is both safe and idempotent (same payload always yields the same entry, deduplicatable).' But idempotency in the f(f(x))=f(x) sense means retrying the write produces the same log state as a single write—i.e., the log contains one entry, not duplicate identical entries. A deterministic-hash writer still appends a new (content-identical) entry on every retry, growing the log. The parenthetical 'deduplicatable' reveals that collapse to a single entry is a processor-side operation, not a writer property. Thus the target conflates determinism (same input → same output) with idempotency (applying twice = applying once), undermining the very distinction between 'safe' and 'idempotent' that the problem asks it to clarify.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}